---
import BlogArticle from '../../components/BlogArticle.astro';
import BlogCode from '../../components/BlogCode.astro';

const contents = [
  { id: 'collision', label: 'Two branches, one cloud' },
  { id: 'pair', label: 'One volume per worktree' },
  { id: 'side-by-side', label: 'See both runtimes side by side' },
  { id: 'agents', label: 'The same trick for coding agents' },
  { id: 'cleanup', label: 'Clean up after the merge' },
];
const worktreeCmd = `# Terminal 1: the feature branch
git worktree add ../repo-feature-search feat/search-index
cd ../repo-feature-search
localcloud start --local-only --data-volume feat-search
eval "$(localcloud env --data-volume feat-search)"

# Terminal 2: the hotfix, on its own volume and ports
git worktree add ../repo-hotfix fix/billing-export
cd ../repo-hotfix
localcloud start --local-only --data-volume hotfix-billing --accept-dynamic-ports
eval "$(localcloud env --data-volume hotfix-billing)"`;
const statusCmd = `localcloud status --data-volume hotfix-billing`;
const statusOut = `[OK] LocalCloud container is running
Data volume      hotfix-billing
Origin           managed
Ports            5406, 5508-5514 -> 5380, 5382-5383, 5388-5389, 5392-5393, 5406
URL              http://127.0.0.1:5508`;
const cleanupCmd = `localcloud stop --data-volume hotfix-billing
git worktree remove ../repo-hotfix`;
---

<BlogArticle
  slug="parallel-git-worktrees-localcloud"
  kind="Walkthrough"
  deck="Git worktrees let you keep a feature, a review and a hotfix checked out at once. That only helps if each one has somewhere separate to run. Give every worktree its own local cloud and they stop stepping on each other."
  primary={{ href: '/docs/configuration/#runtime-selection', label: 'Runtime selection guide' }}
  secondary={{ href: '/docs/', label: 'Quick start' }}
  {contents}
  outcome="Two worktrees, each with its own LocalCloud runtime, data and ports, running at the same time without sharing any state."
  needs="a Git repository"
  next={{ posts: ['reproduce-gcp-incidents-locally', 'why-ai-agents-need-local-cloud'], doc: { href: '/docs/configuration/#runtime-selection', label: 'Configuration: how a command picks its runtime' } }}
>
  <section>
    <h2 id="collision">Two branches, one cloud</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">You are halfway through a schema change on a feature branch when a billing bug needs fixing now. Worktrees make the code side easy: a second checkout, a second terminal, no stashing.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">The cloud side is where it goes wrong. Both checkouts point at the same dev project or the same container, so the hotfix runs its tests against tables your feature branch has half migrated. One branch's cleanup deletes the other's fixtures, and both sets of results become suspect.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="pair">One volume per worktree</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">LocalCloud identifies a runtime by its data volume. Start with a different <code>--data-volume</code> name and you get a separate container with its own storage, databases and queues. The second runtime adds <code>--accept-dynamic-ports</code> so it takes free ports instead of fighting over the defaults.</p>
    <BlogCode label="Two worktrees, two runtimes" code={worktreeCmd} copy="copy-blog-worktree-cmd" />
    <p class="mt-4">The <code>eval</code> line matters most. It exports that runtime's endpoints into that terminal only, so tests in each worktree reach their own cloud without any change to the code.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="side-by-side">See both runtimes side by side</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Ask for the status of either one by name. The hotfix runtime shows the ports it was given in place of the defaults:</p>
    <BlogCode label="Check the hotfix runtime" code={statusCmd} copy="copy-blog-worktree-status" />
    <BlogCode label="Output (trimmed)" code={statusOut} />
    <p class="mt-4">Run the feature branch's migrations, then the hotfix's test suite. Neither sees the other. Each runtime also has its own web console at the URL shown, which is a quick way to confirm which data you are looking at.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="agents">The same trick for coding agents</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">This matters more when the second worktree belongs to an agent. An agent fixing a bug in parallel will create, delete and reset resources freely, which is what you want as long as they aren't yours. Start its runtime with its own volume name, hand it that worktree, and review the result when it is done.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="cleanup">Clean up after the merge</h2>
    <BlogCode label="Stop the runtime and remove the worktree" code={cleanupCmd} copy="copy-blog-worktree-cleanup" />
    <p class="mt-4">Stopping keeps the volume, so a branch you come back to next week still has its data. When the branch is gone for good, remove the stopped container and its volume with Docker; the <a href="/docs/configuration/#stop-reset-and-uninstall">lifecycle section</a> lists what each command keeps.</p>
  </section>
</BlogArticle>
